2026-06-30 15:21:49,432 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-cd7aa88a-3b6d-4756-934c-11ef88a0100b', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to sample preparation and digestion.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: cleavage agent details\nsdrf_column: comment[cleavage agent details]\nontology: MS:1001044 (PSI-MS)\ndescription: Enzyme or chemical used for protein digestion.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS ontology terms. Multiple enzymes are common in XL-MS.\n  If multiple, return a list.\n  Common terms:\n    Trypsin (MS:1001251) — cleaves K,R (not before P)\n    Lys-C (MS:1001309) — cleaves K\n    Lys-C/P (MS:1001309) — cleaves K including before P\n    Asp-N (MS:1001303) — cleaves N-terminal to D\n    Glu-C (MS:1001917) — cleaves C-terminal to E, D\n    Chymotrypsin (MS:1001306) — cleaves F,Y,W,L\n    No cleavage (MS:1001955)\n    Unspecific cleavage (MS:1001956)\n  Format each as: NT=<enzyme name>;AC=<MS accession>\n\nterm: label\nsdrf_column: comment[label]\nontology: PRIDE:0000514, MS\ndescription: Labeling strategy used for quantification.\ninstructions: Most XL-MS is label-free. Use:\n  NT=label free sample;AC=MS:1002038 — for label-free\n  NT=SILAC;AC=MS:1002038 — for SILAC\n  NT=iTRAQ4plex;AC=PRIDE:0000114 — for iTRAQ 4-plex\n  NT=TMT6plex;AC=PRIDE:0000116 — for TMT\n  If not stated, assume label free.\n\nterm: alkylation reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[alkylation reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000598\ndescription: Chemical reagent used for cysteine alkylation (typically before digestion).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Common: iodoacetamide (IAA), iodoacetic acid,\n  N-ethylmaleimide (NEM), acrylamide. If iodoacetamide, use "iodoacetamide".\n\nterm: fractionation method\nsdrf_column: comment[fractionation method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000550\ndescription: Peptide fractionation method used before LC-MS/MS (distinct from crosslink\n  enrichment — this is standard proteomics fractionation).\ninstructions: Return null if no pre-LC fractionation. Examples: "high-pH reversed-phase\n  chromatography", "basic reversed-phase LC", "SCX fractionation", "HILIC".\n\nterm: modification parameters\nsdrf_column: comment[modification parameters]\nontology: MS:1001055, Unimod, PSI-MOD\ndescription: Post-translational modifications searched in the database search.\noutput_format: List of modifications, each formatted as:\n  NT=<mod name>;AC=<Unimod accession>;TA=<target amino acid>;MT=<Fixed or Variable>\ninstructions: Always include carbamidomethyl cysteine if IAA alkylation was used:\n  NT=Carbamidomethyl;AC=UNIMOD:4;TA=C;MT=Fixed\n  Always include oxidation of methionine if searched:\n  NT=Oxidation;AC=UNIMOD:21;TA=M;MT=Variable\n  Include crosslinker mono-link (dead-end) modifications if mentioned.\n  Return a list even if only one modification.\n\nterm: fraction identifier\nsdrf_column: comment[fraction identifier]\nontology: MS:1000858\ndescription: Fraction number for fractionated samples.\ninstructions: Describe whether the dataset uses fractionation and what the fractionation\n  scheme is (e.g. "12 SEC fractions per replicate", "no fractionation").\n  The actual integer values are assigned per-file; describe the scheme here.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nFor comment[modification parameters], "value" should be a list of formatted strings.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nMultiplexed isobaric quantitative cross-linking reveals drug-induced interactome changes in breast cancer cells\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nThe study of protein structures and interactions is critical to understand their function. Chemical cross-linking of proteins with mass spectrometry (XL-MS) is a rapidly developing structural biology technique able to provide valuable insight into protein conformations and interactions, even as they exist within their native cellular environment. Quantitative analysis of cross-links can reveal protein conformational and interaction changes that occur as a result of altered biological states, environmental conditions, or pharmacological perturbations. Our laboratory recently developed an isobaric quantitative Protein Interaction Reporter (iqPIR) cross-linking strategy for comparative interactome studies. This strategy relies on isotope encoded chemical cross-linkers that have the same molecular mass, yet produce unique and specific isotope signatures upon fragmentation in the mass spectrometer which can be used for quantitative analysis of cross-linked peptides. The initial set of iqPIR molecules allowed for binary comparisons. Here, we describe the in vivo application of an extended set of six iqPIR reagents (6-plex iqPIR) allowing multiplexed quantitative interactome analysis of up to six biological samples in a single LC-MS acquisition. Multiplexed iqPIR is demonstrated on MCF-7 breast cancer cells treated with five different Hsp90 inhibitors revealing large scale protein conformational and interaction changes specific to the molecular class of the inhibitors.\nGraphical Abstract\n\n[METHODS]\nMETHODS\nCell culture and harvesting\nMCF-7 cells were treated with different concentrations of Hsp90 inhibitors. Complete information on drug treatment and IC50 measurements is made available in Supporting Information.\nIn vivo 6-plex iqPIR cross-linking\nIn vivo randomized cross-linking of six biological samples of MCF-7 cells with 3 replicates is described in Supporting Information. Information on the synthesis and molecular details of the 6-plex iqPIR cross-linkers is made available in the accompanying manuscript by Chavez et al. .\nSample preparation\nSix independent cross-linked cell pellets were prepared as previously described and merged into a multiplexed sample. Complete information is available in Supporting Information.\nLC-MS/MS analysis of cross-linked peptides\nLC-MS/MS was performed as previously described and complete information is made available in Supporting Information.\nData analysis\nIn silico analysis of the 6-plex iqPIR qXL-MS data was done as described by Chavez et al. . Further details are made available in Supporting Information.\n\n[RESULTS]\nRESULTS AND DISCUSSION\nThe ability to probe protein structures and interactions from within their native cellular environment in an untargeted fashion and on a systems-wide scale will serve as an important tool in structural and systems biology research. Here, we demonstrate the 6-plex iqPIR qXL-MS strategy in vivo on six biological samples of breast cancer cells (MCF-7; Figure S1), with 3 different replicates, treated with 5 different Hsp90 inhibitors and a vehicle control, to assess large-scale drug induced changes in protein conformations and interactions. The specific Hsp90 inhibitors used here were chosen as they bind to different domains of the same primary protein target and are therefore useful for demonstrating drug class specific protein conformational and interaction changes. Specifically, we chose the canonical Hsp90 inhibitors targeting the ATP binding pocket in the N-terminal domain (NTD; 17-AAG, XL-888, and ganetespib), gambogic acid which binds to the middle domain (MD) of Hsp90, and the C-terminal domain (CTD)-binding novobiocin. This strategy allowed the labeling of six different cell conditions, including untreated control, multiplexed in one final sample before the proteolytic digestion and further sample preparation and LC-MS analysis. Multiplexing samples has several benefits including requiring less starting material from each condition, streamlining sample preparation, minimizing unintended variance, reducing LC-MS analysis time and ultimately simplifying data analysis. Generation of 6-plex iqPIR qXL-MS data required that we develop new computational strategies for automated analysis of the data, which are described in detail in the accompanying manuscript. After in silico analysis, a total of 2,576 non-redundant cross-links were identified for the combined 3 replicates, with estimated 1% FDR, with 42% inter-links (inter-protein links), corresponding to 2,094 site pairs and 622 protein pairs. The complete cross-linking information for the 3 replicates analyzed separately and for the combined data is made available in Figures S2–4, Table S3, and online at http://xlinkdb.gs.washington.edu/xlinkdb/MCF-7_6plex_Bruce.php. In addition to computing drug treatment cross-link log2 ratios independently for reach replicate (Table S3), they were also calculated from the combined quantified ions of all three replicates. We note that having multiple quantitative signals from each iqPIR channel present in each assigned mass spectrum results in high quantitative coverage of the data. For instance, 92.5% of all cross-links (2,379 out of 2,576 total) were quantified and had log2 ratios (i.e., each drug treatment versus the untreated control) assigned throughout the five drug treatments. The 95% confidence intervals of the combined drug treatment log2 ratios thereby reflect the reproducibility of the quantitation among the contributing replicates. Indeed, the multiplexed iqPIR data displayed reproducible quantitative results with 1,767 (69%) of the cross-links quantified across all six channels, with 95% confidence intervals no greater than log2 1. Moreover, approximately 40% of the cross-linked peptides have log2 ratios ranging from −0.5 to 0.5 across different preparations. This can be considered the range of non-changing links containing all experimental and biological variability. This demonstrates that reproducibility was observed across six different biological samples within a single multiplexed sample as a significant number of cross-links (723 out of 1,767) have ratios that do not change between the different preparations. In fact, with 6 biological replicates in HeLa 6-plex sample over 90% of the log2 ratios with 95% confidence intervals less than 1 fall within the range of −0.5 to 0.5, with similar variance throughout the 15 ratios (Figure S5a&c). Examining the same unchanging range in the MCF-7 dataset demonstrates the unchanging cross-links have a similar variance throughout the 5 ratios (treatment compared to control), based on the standard deviations (Figure S5b&d). Figure S5e shows the low variability among log2 ratios of housekeeping keratins KRT8 and KRT18 in HeLa 6-plex and MCF-7 6-plex samples. Cross-links with log2ratios outside of this range (log2 −0.5 to 0.5) quantified with 95% confidence intervals of less than 1 are changing due to effects of the drug treatments. Additionally, we examined the quantitative reproducibility of cross-links having distinct masses, which span the same residue pairs as they represent independent measurements of the same structural features (Figure S6), and over 90% of log2 ratios varied below log2 0.2 within cross-linked peptide groups with methionine oxidation or missed cleavages, throughout the 6 channels.\nThe iqPIR approach can also be used to measure protein level abundance changes by quantifying dead-end cross-links, where one reactive group of the cross-linker reacts with water and the other with a reactive residue site within a protein, in combination with intra-protein cross-links, as was previously demonstrated. Here, a total of 2,092 dead-ends and intra-links combined were quantified throughout the 6 samples, with zero missing values and 95% confidence intervals for the log2 ratios less ≤ 1, which provides relative protein level log2 ratios for 282 proteins. Specifically, from 1,767 cross-links quantified across all six channels, with zero missing values and having 95% confidence intervals for the log2 ratios ≤ 1, 1,492 cross-linked peptides had protein quantitation assigned with the same parameters (zero missing values and having 95% confidence intervals for the log2 ratios ≤ 1), corresponding to 264 proteins with cross-linked peptides. Having both protein level and cross-link level quantitative values allows for distinguishing conformational and protein interaction level changes from protein abundance level changes.\nGlobally, the cross-links displayed distinct quantitative patterns in each of the 6 iqPIR channels. To evaluate the data for drug specific information, we compared data across the five different drug treatments using a set of confidently quantified cross-links (95% confidence interval for log2 ratio ≤ 1). Hierarchical clustering of the cross-link ratios using an average pairwise Euclidean distance indicates a high degree of similarity among cross-link ratios of NTD binding drugs (17-AAG, XL-888, and ganetespib), which cluster separately from gambogic acid and novobiocin ratios – MD and CTD binding inhibitors, respectively (Figure 2a). The high level of agreement in quantitative values across the three biological samples treated with NTD binding drugs demonstrates that reproducibility is achievable with the multiplexed iqPIR approach for in vivo cross-linked samples. Differences in the quantitative profiles of cross-link ratios exist despite the fact that the global distribution of cross-link ratios from the different drug treatments display a large degree of overlap (Figure 2c). While all distributions in Figure 2c are centered near a log2 ratio of zero, the distributions of novobiocin and gambogic acid are overall wider than that of the other drugs. The overlap of non-changing ratios across the 6 biological samples indicates reproducibility in the quantitative measurements as it is reflective of background variability. Quantitative changes beyond this are able to be clearly distinguished and are evident based on the quantitative data clustering according to the primary domain target of the drugs. Furthermore, linear correlation analysis of cross-link ratios between drug classes revealed higher correlation and measured coefficients of determination (R2) between NTD targeting drugs when compared with MD-binding gambogic acid and CTD-binding novobiocin (Figure 2b). Thus on a global level drug class specific information was revealed within the large-scale qXL-MS data.\nAfter assessing large-scale changes on MCF-7 cross-links with drug treatments using the 6-plex iqPIR cross-linking, we further investigated specific changes involving Hsp90, the primary target for the inhibitors used here (Figure 3). Hsp90 is a cytosolic heat shock protein with two different isoforms: the inducible Hsp90α and the constitutively expressed Hsp90β. Specifically, a total of 61 cross-links were identified for Hsp90α/β, with 23% inter-links, corresponding to 46 site pairs. Forty-four cross-link pairs had log2 ratios assigned for all 5 drug treatments (zero missing ratio values), within the 95% confidence interval threshold ≤ 1. Due to the homology and sequence similarity between Hsp90α and Hsp90β (Figure S7), the two proteins have corresponding lysine residues that were found cross-linked in this study (Table 1). Comparison of the subset of Hsp90 cross-links reveals more distinguishing quantitative profiles highlighting the similarity of the NTD inhibitors, distinct from MD inhibitor gambogic acid and CTD inhibitor novobiocin (Figure 3a,c&d). Specifically, Hsp90-related cross-link ratios resulting from novobiocin treatment are mostly not changing, while those from gambogic acid and NTD inhibitors are either increasing or display no change. Evaluating the Hsp90 isoform specific information in more detail revealed that, after treatment with inhibitors that bind to the NTD, cross-link log2 ratios are mainly increasing for Hsp90α homodimers (Figure 3c). This is an expected result based on induction of Hsp90α abundance and the general heat shock response induced by NTD inhibitors. Overall, these multiplexed iqPIR qXL-MS results agree with previous SILAC-based qXL-MS findings on HeLa cells treated with NTD-binding drugs 17-AAG and XL-888, and CTD-binding novobiocin. One example is the Hsp90β/α heterodimer (FYEAFSK435NLK_FYEQFSK443NIK), which has increasing ratios after treatment with NTD-binding drugs and gambogic acid, but decreasing log2 ratio after novobiocin treatment (Figure 3c). As the CTD is important in dimerization of Hsp90 (Figure 3b), this observation is consistent with previous reports that CTD inhibitors disrupt Hsp90 dimerization. The lysine residues K435 and K443 are located in the middle domain of Hsp90β and α, respectively (Figure 3b&c). The binding of 17-AAG, XL-888, and ganetespib to the ATP pocket located within the NTD of Hsp90α leads to increased Hsp90α homodimer levels, agreeing with the results found here.\nThe MS1 and MS2 spectra for the Hsp90β_Hsp90α heterodimer (cross-linked peptides FYEAFSK435NLK_FYEQFSK443NIK) are shown in Figure 4. The MS1 spectrum shows the isotope envelope of the precursor ion, comprised of all 6 isobaric cross-linked peptide ions. MS2 spectrum reveals fragments ions, including the released reporter ions, released intact peptide A/B (FYEAFSK435NLK_FYEQFSK443NIK) ions, and backbone y-type and b-type fragment ions from peptides A/B. The observed isotope encoded peaks from released peptide and backbone fragment ions from each channel are used for cross-link quantitation (Figure 4b&d). Due to signal overlap resultant from the isotope spacing between the iqPIR channels 2 Da (or 1 Da between 818 and 816 channels) the observed iqPIR isotope envelope requires deconvolution of the signal into the six channels. This is accomplished using information on natural isotope abundances as described in detail in the accompanying manuscript by Chavez et al. . Reporter ion signal is not used for quantitation due to ratio compression effects resultant from co-isolation of multiple precursor ions, as observed previously. Since there is no full length structural model of human Hsp90α in the PDB, the Hsp90β K435_K443 Hsp90β/α heterodimer is represented on a homologous Hsp90β structure (PBD 5FWK; Figure 4c). Overall, the cross-linking results obtained here with in vivo application of 6-plex iqPIR technologies to MCF-7 cells demonstrate the first large-scale quantitative cellular interactome changes observed with multiple drugs.\nAlthough the three NTD-binding inhibitors had similar effects on Hsp90 cross-link ratios as expected (Figure 3), multiple cross-link ratios for the ADP/ATP translocase 2 are decreasing after treatment with gambogic acid (MD-binding), ganetespib and XL-888 (NTD binding), differing from 17-AAG (NTD-binding) ratios, which remain unaltered with treatment (Figure 5). Thus, multiplex cross-linking results can highlight similarities across different drug classes as well. ADP/ATP translocase 2 (ADT2_HUMAN) is a mitochondrial transporter of ADP and ATP existing in two states: the mitochondrial matrix-open m-state and the cytoplasm-open c-state. The Jwalk Solvent Accessible Surface Distance (SASD) calculations between cross-linked lysines reveal that the cross-link between lysine residues K272_K23 (AFFK272GAWSNVLR_DFLAGGVAAAISK23TAVAPIER) is only compatible with the m-state (31.43 Å compared with 66.61 Å of c-state; Figure 5a). We note that the lysine residue K23 is embedded in the ATP-binding site of ADT2. The difference in distances between the two states along with decreasing ratios of the K272_K23 link in the m-state suggests that treatment with XL-888, ganetespib, and gambogic acid decreases m-state levels and shifts ADT2 conformational equilibrium towards the c-state. This could potentially result from the binding of ATP-mimetic XL-888 and ganetespib to the ATP-binding pocket of ADT2 in the intermembrane space, interrupting the transport of ATP by the m-state and shifting the protein equilibrium to the ADP-bound c-state. Accordingly, it has been previously reported a loss of mitochondrial membrane potential after treatment of vemurafenib resistance cell lines with XL-888. Evidence shows that ganetespib induces apoptosis in MCF-7 cells by mitochondria-mediated pathways of cell death. Gambogic acid induced mitochondrial outer membrane permeabilization in MCF-7 cells, disrupting the ATP transport. On the other hand, no change in K272_K23 cross-link level was observed after treatment with 17-AAG (Figure 5b; log2 ratio between −0.5 and 0.5), which is also an ADP mimetic. These results illustrate how the 6-plex qXL-MS information can reveal unexpected patterns in protein conformations other than the primary drug target.\nMoreover, Figure 5b & S8 show the 6-plex iqPIR dead-end-based estimation of ADT2 abundance in the samples after treatment with Hsp90 inhibitors. Many of the cross-links involving ADT2 display similar quantitative values as the protein abundance level changes, indicating that the inhibitors gambogic acid and ganetespib resulted in decreased protein levels for ADT2. However, the inhibitors novobiocin, XL-888, and 17-AAG did not alter ADT2 levels. Cross-linked peptides K147_K272 and K94_K23 display quantitative values that differ from the protein abundance levels, thus indicative of conformational changes. The protein abundance information is available on the cross-linking interaction table online at http://xlinkdb.gs.washington.edu/xlinkdb/MCF-7_6plex_Bruce.php.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
